{"highest":12,"ids":{"RQREB-1 is not covered: DSRACE-1 covers concurrent-claim detection by reading back GitHub comments (notes), not by fetching the GitHub issue itself; \"reads back all trusted concurrent claim notes\" and \"re-reads the issue\" are different operations, and no SPEC demands that the issue's metadata or labels are read after adding the label — After adding the label, a run re-reads the issue [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: a SPEC demands that after successfully adding the label, the claim reads back the GitHub issue itself (its data — labels, state, or metadata — not only comments or notes), and the read occurs after the synchronous holder record is written]":"DSFND-1","UNCLEAR RQCMT-1: the issue now answers it: the issue says the standard progress comment does not exist at claim time — it is started in the background after `pipeline.start`, which never fires for a refused run — so at claim time the arbitration note is the only GitHub comment the loser posts; \"marks its progress comment as not started\" therefore refers to that note — Today the progress comment is started in the background, after the issue lock (zbuild_issue_lock_acquire, core/pipeline/runner.sh) and pipeline.start, so at claim time a run has no comment of its own to compare [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: the UNCLEAR RQCMT-1 line is gone, and the work follows the issue's words where they differ from its assumption]":"DSFND-2","DSRACE-2: 1 part to fix\nDSRACE-2[code]: Only the run whose concurrent claim note is first (by created_at, then id) returns {\"acquired\": true}; all others return {\"acquired\": false} covers: RQTIE-2, RQTIE-1\n  Given: two or more concurrent claim notes exist for the same issue within the detection window\n  When: each run compares the notes and identifies the first\n  Then: the run whose note has the earliest created_at returns {\"acquired\": true} and deletes its note [FIX IN DESIGN: the issue requires the first-claiming run to proceed; it says nothing about the winner deleting its arbitration note — ACCEPTED IF: the Then must state only that the winning run returns {\"acquired\": true}, without committing to any note-deletion step the issue does not ask for]\n  And: all other runs return {\"acquired\": false, \"holder\": \"<winner_holder>\", \"race_lost\": true}":"DSFND-3","DSRACE-4: 1 part to fix\nDSRACE-4[done]: A run whose claim_coordinator_claim returns {\"acquired\": false} never removes the zbuild:claimed label covers: RQLBL-1 evidence: core/claim/claim.sh | [[ -n \"$issue\" ]] || return 0 [FIX IN DESIGN: when `claim_coordinator_claim` returns `{\"acquired\": false}`, the existing code calls `_zbuild_claim_takeover` for any cross-machine holder (lines 119–123 of `zbuild_claim_acquire`); inside `_zbuild_claim_takeover`, if the takeover hook returns `taken_over: true, claimed: true`, the code sets `_ZBUILD_CLAIM_ACQUIRED=\"$issue\"` (line 197 of `_zbuild_claim_takeover`), after which `zbuild_claim_release` will remove the label — so `_ZBUILD_CLAIM_ACQUIRED` is NOT guaranteed never-set after `acquired: false` in the existing code; that guarantee requires DSRACE-5's `race_lost: true` skip to be in place first: core/claim/claim.sh | _ZBUILD_CLAIM_ACQUIRED=\"$issue\" — it is [code], not [done] — ACCEPTED IF: DSRACE-4 must be [code], conditioned on DSRACE-5, with evidence showing the stale-takeover path is unreachable for a race-lost response]\n  When: claim_coordinator_claim returns {\"acquired\": false, ...}\n  Then: _ZBUILD_CLAIM_ACQUIRED is never set, zbuild_claim_release is a no-op, and the label is not removed":"DSFND-4","Decision: the design's decisions go against the issue: RQTIE-1 requires that when two runs claim an issue at the same moment, exactly one proceeds — no qualification. The design's outside assumptions explicitly describe a scenario where both competing runs complete their note-post-and-read cycle before the other run's note has propagated, both see only their own note, and both return `{\"acquired\": true}`. Both runs therefore proceed, which cannot be true together with RQTIE-1. — has propagated, both see only their own note and both return `{\"acquired\": true}`. Closing [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: The design does not accept a scenario where both runs return `{\"acquired\": true}` and proceed; or RQTIE-1 is itself qualified in the issue to permit a narrow propagation-window exception.]":"DSFND-5","DSRACE-15 covers core/claim/claim.sh, which is not a requirement or limit intake listed — name in its covers: the requirement it delivers, or take the SPEC out: a SPEC traces to what intake listed":"DSFND-6","DSRACE-15 covers plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh, which is not a requirement or limit intake listed — name in its covers: the requirement it delivers, or take the SPEC out: a SPEC traces to what intake listed":"DSFND-7","DSRACE-9: 1 part to fix\nDSRACE-9[code]: If GitHub's reply to the record POST has no created_at, the claim removes its record and the label and does not acquire covers: RQFAIL-1, RQTST-4\n  Given: the fake GitHub answers the record POST without created_at\n  When: the claim runs\n  Then: the answer is not acquired\n  And: the record and the label are no longer on the issue\n  And: no value from the run's own clock is used [FIX IN DESIGN: The last And, \"no value from the run's own clock is used\", is not something a test can see after the When. The earlier Thens already show the observable result: the claim is not acquired and the record and label are gone. — ACCEPTED IF: a test could observe that, with no time in GitHub's reply, the claim fails closed (not acquired, record and label removed) whatever the run's own clock says.]":"DSFND-8","UNCLEAR RQTIE-3: the issue now answers it: The issue offers the own holder record as one of two allowed options. The later requirements fix it as the one used: R4 says \"posting the holder record\" and R6 says \"After posting its holder record … Reading the holder records back is the re-read.\" Reading (a) is therefore right. — After posting its holder record, a run does not re-read the issue's labels. Reading the holder records back is the re-read. [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: the UNCLEAR RQTIE-3 line is gone, and the work follows the issue's words where they differ from its assumption]":"DSFND-9","Decision: the design's decisions commit to something no requirement needs: The issue's scope lists three code files: `plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh`, `core/claim/claim.sh` and `core/pipeline/runner.sh`. No requirement asks for a new `lib/race.sh`. Moving the helpers to keep `plugin.sh` under 500 lines is a design choice that the issue does not need. — so the race helpers move to `lib/race.sh` (as `takeover.sh` is a lib) to keep `plugin.sh` [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: Everything the change adds sits in the files the issue's scope names, or the issue's scope names the new file.]":"DSFND-10","Decision: the design's decisions go against the issue: RQCMT-1 and R1 require the later run's progress comment to say it did not start. The design only rewrites the loser's holder record, which is a separate comment, to \"not started — concurrent claim\". It never touches the progress comment, and it names no change to `core/pipeline/runner.sh`, where the progress comment is started. — Loser: PATCHes its record to \"not started — concurrent claim: `<winner>` was first\" [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: The run that loses the tie-break has its progress comment say it did not start, as well as exiting without removing the label.]":"DSFND-11","Decision: the design does not handle an outside assumption: The design relies on reading the issue's comments once and finding every holder record in that read. It does not say what happens when the GitHub comments API pages its results, so a record could fall outside the first page. It also does not say what happens when the loser's PATCH of its record fails, or when the delete of the record on release fails. A stale record left behind would be counted by a later run. — It then reads the issue's comments **once** (RQREAD-1). [FIX IN DESIGN: ACCEPTED IF: The decisions say how the one read of holder records covers all comments, and how a failed PATCH or delete is handled (fail clearly, or why it is safe to assume).]":"DSFND-12"}}
